{"count": 20457, "next": "https://api.acmi.net.au/creators/?page=537", "previous": "https://api.acmi.net.au/creators/?page=535", "results": [{"id": 80042, "name": "Cindy Cowan", "roles_in_work": [{"id": 2267250, "title": "Very bad things", "work_id": 92905, "role": "co-producer", "role_id": 915, "is_primary": true}], "also_known_as": "", "date_of_birth": null, "date_of_death": "", "places_of_operation": [{"id": 1, "name": "United States of America", "slug": "united-states-of-america"}], "biography": "Cindy Cowan (born May 7, 1959) is an American film producer and songwriter.\nCowan graduated from Tulane University and attended graduate courses toward a master's degree in psychology at Harvard. Her producing career began as a producer and writer for a CBS News affiliate in Miami, Florida.In 1995, Cowan co-founded Initial Entertainment Group (IEG) with Graham King. During her tenure as president, IEG had successes that included: an Emmy nomination for Rent-A-Kid starring Leslie Nielsen; Emmy, Golden Globe and People's Choice nominations for If These Walls Could Talk; a United Nations Award for Savior starring Dennis Quaid; and Oscar-winning Traffic starring Michael Douglas. Additional projects produced by Cowan included Very Bad Things and Robert Altman's Dr. T & the Women.\nIn 1999, Cowan sold her two-thirds stake in IEG to Splendid Films and started Cindy Cowan Entertainment where she produced Scorched, Fifty Dead Men Walking, and Red Lights starring Robert De Niro and Sigourney Weaver.As a songwriter and lyricist, Cowan has written songs for film and television; an album title track for Engelbert Humperdinck, a chart topping hit \"This Love is Forever\" for Howard Hewett, and several songs for Pamala Stanley.", "biography_author": "Wikidata", "date_of_biography": "2023-08-01T14:27:34.824928+10:00", "external_links": [{"id": 1827, "uri": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cindy_Cowan"}], "uuid": "1ad4ce06-c904-464b-87da-ac98de5aa81a", "source": {"name": "Vernon", "slug"